1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,438
PART 2 Woman: I'm very pleased to welcome this evening's guest speaker,

2
00:00:06,438 --> 00:00:13,950
Mark Logan, who's going to tell us about the recent transformation of Minster Park.

3
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:15,610
Over to you, Mark.

4
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:20,950
Mark: Thank you. I'm sure you're all familiar with Minster Park.

5
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:25,076
It's been a feature of the city for well over a century,

6
00:00:25,076 --> 00:00:29,890
and has been the responsibility of the city council for most of that time.

7
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:36,162
What perhaps isn't so well known is the origin of the park:

8
00:00:36,162 --> 00:00:39,680
unlike many public parks that started in private ownership

9
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:44,525
as the garden of a large house,

10
00:00:44,525 --> 00:00:53,410
for instance, Minster was some waste land, which people living nearby started planting with flowers in 1892.

11
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:57,003
It was unclear who actually owned the land,

12
00:00:57,003 --> 00:01:00,920
and this wasn't settled until 20 years later

13
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:03,740
when the council took possession of it.

14
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:08,500
You may have noticed the statue near one of the entrances.

15
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:14,740
It's of Diane Gosforth, who played a key role in the history of the park.

16
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,560
Once the council had become the legal owner

17
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:21,000
it planned to sell the land for housing.

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,540
Many local people wanted it to remain a place that everyone could go to

19
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,598
to enjoy the fresh air

20
00:01:29,598 --> 00:01:35,480
and natural environment- remember the park is in a densely populated residential area.

21
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:39,120
Diane Gosforth was one of those people

22
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:42,062
and she organized petitions and demonstrations,

23
00:01:42,062 --> 00:01:49,150
which eventually made the council change its mind about the future of the land.

24
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:53,517
Soon after this the First World War broke out,

25
00:01:53,517 --> 00:01:58,150
in 1914, and most of the park was dug up

26
00:01:58,150 --> 00:02:02,250
and planted with vegetables, which were sold locally.

27
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:07,508
At one stage the army considered taking it over for troop exercises

28
00:02:07,508 --> 00:02:10,985
and got as far as contacting the city council,

29
00:02:11,748 --> 00:02:16,270
then decided the park was too small to be of use

30
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:19,870
There were occasional public meetings during the war

31
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:24,000
in an area that had been retained as grass.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:33,172
After the war, the park was turned back more or less to how it had been before 1914,

33
00:02:33,172 --> 00:02:36,230
and continued almost unchanged until recently.

34
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:41,738
Plans for transforming it were drawn up at various times,

35
00:02:41,738 --> 00:02:46,930
most recently in 2013, though they were revised in 2015

36
00:02:46,930 --> 00:02:50,000
before any work had started.

37
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,010
The changes finally got going in 2016, and were finished on schedule last year.

38
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:04,970
OK, let me tell you about some of the changes that have been made

39
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:07,810
and some things that have been retained.

40
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:13,663
If you look at this map, you'll see the familiar outline of the park,

41
00:03:13,663 --> 00:03:16,090
with the river forming the northern boundary

42
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:20,770
and a gate in each of the other three walls.

43
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:23,984
The statue of Diane Gosforth has been moved:

44
00:03:23,984 --> 00:03:27,470
it used to be close to the south gate

45
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:32,018
but it's now immediately to the north of the lily pond,

46
00:03:32,018 --> 00:03:37,240
almost in the centre of the park, which makes it much more visible.

47
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,113
There's a new area of wooden sculptures,

48
00:03:40,113 --> 00:03:47,090
which are on the river bank, where the path from the east gate makes a sharp bend.

49
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:52,000
There are two areas that are particularly intended for children.

50
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,891
The playground has been enlarged and improved,

51
00:03:54,891 --> 00:04:01,500
and that's between the river and the path that leads from the pond to the river.

52
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:09,500
Then there's a new maze, a circular series of paths, separated by low hedges.

53
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,830
That's near the west gate

54
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:18,500
you go north from there towards the river and then turn left to reach it.

55
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:22,430
There have been tennis courts in the park for many years

56
00:04:22,870 --> 00:04:26,920
and they've been doubled, from four to eight.

57
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:30,250
They're still in the south-west corner of the park

58
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:33,700
where there's a right-angle bend in the path.

59
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:38,530
Something else I'd like to mention is the new fitness area.

60
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:44,130
This is right next to the lily pond on the same side as the west gate.

61
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:47,350
Now, as you're all gardeners

62
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:52,000
I'm sure you'll like to hear about the plants that have been chosen for the park.
